lution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-7252/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-7252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-31364"] :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-31424"] :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-27539/selection/1d52fbf31564d373a409f3f8765874ebe998f9438ab747c3d7bd3ac4ec7cb27b" :answer/id #uuid "1087f994-7004-55b0-a4ab-3757e9fbaec9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6ba95e4c4a7224797d9a1b8eecec20c86babce60bad95c67565e1227204c6e8a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27539/selection/b44a72ef813959c5b0303ebbde8db7e7d016d606b8ebcd2d5e3bbe21a6b44ead" :answer/id #uuid "76c114a3-95e5-543f-8c8c-55dc719f7809" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/1c6d66581178dc2e49c2b8b2b133224a7863efd416f10ac179e9fa7911880618.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27539/selection/cb10a5f5fc2e54f788a8fd97c31ab1874741eac00c5ff55607864faae12eeb24" :answer/id #uuid "0c788c23-82b6-5747-be94-15fa27b605c5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/1fa60a8c5fb3fe49fd53c4ebcb89e0e430bb942146a7a0356ce2378d37c6b701.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27539/selection/4c8810ff24d36109938612dd6c67bbca309119fa17e32a4fed6154490f868035" :answer/id #uuid "56c094fc-87f6-5f75-900a-fcacf70759c2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/ma