6" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-31416/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-31416"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection/7c2fec16fd7ae02af1a8818a9096a676afc42249e7e68d108c3f64859048a45f" :answer/id #uuid "18e25cc2-6ead-5cfc-90e0-f04a30550817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection/bc99aa5f3ee62c40cb834b274ce88683dbffd8fee8404db2b536df0189aa9cdc" :answer/id #uuid "5e1fb1ca-d7f4-5dfb-ace3-3b8c42de22d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection/82c06270a9acd7d2cccf259d262fdfacdb0b322c73eeaa437f1cf50ba1ae5775" :answer/id #uuid "ff0222fb-2c06-5e14-942e-057278763256" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection/c5d39364ee5ba1c4c491f26ca6eaf96ec42a4805e0fae6421599854cb64c2a0c" :answer/id #uuid "aa54ecc3-f2c7-58cc-808b-84b5360cdbc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection/0a4d6c1d40cc39b940e2e777304a90dfd4a8e6958e913de83f57d12051966f08" :answer/id #uuid "08bb92f0-da51-5e87-bc92-2c99a54e786b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c10c5ee8-b03c-5652-96d4-546245ae0083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-27542/selection/c5d39364ee5ba1c4c491f26ca6eaf96ec42a4805e0fae6421599854cb64c2a0c" :answer-field/choices ["q-27542/selection/7c2fec16fd7ae02af1a8818a9096a676afc42249e7e68d108c3f64859048a45f" "q-27542/selection/bc99aa5f3ee62c40cb834b274ce88683dbffd8fee8404db2b536df0189aa9cdc" "q-27542/selection/82c06270a9acd7d2cccf259d262fdfacdb0b322c73eeaa437f1cf50ba1ae5775" "q-27542/selection/c5d39364ee5ba1c4c491f26ca6eaf96ec42a4805e0fae6421599854cb64c2a0c" "q-27542/selection/0a4d6c1d40cc39b940e2e777304a90dfd4a8e6958e913de83f57d12051966f08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-27542" :question/id #uuid "8f13e5d3-ff2f-52ec-88f0-51b21c8f112c" :question/math-academy-id "q-27542" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/2a76eaba665b5d7c777ca794ca213bbdc55dfb46b94b49c71679e2c20910add7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Hence, $c=2.5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2.75),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2.75$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.5 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2.5 & =2.75 \\\\ k+2.5 & =2.75 \\\\ k & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=0.25-2.5=-2.25.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=0.25{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-27542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-27542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "8f6465c2-f502-5ca1-a4bb-b74d3f8f658f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "c66fa661-8570-5bb7-a48e-db51a4ad5d99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "c02a727f-56bb-5ad0-beda-3be135f65bfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d4e684c9-b7e5-551d-bec9-fbe1571b47e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "dfe1ea3b-69b8-5ff2-99d7-e79f47f68810" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "476b40d2-fac8-57a3-a5b1-24c906856425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-31435/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-31435/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-31435/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-31435/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-31435/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-31435/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-31435" :question/id #uuid "e11eb136-331c-59a1-bd47-5813f0e994df" :question/math-academy-id "q-31435" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-31435/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-31435"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-27544"] :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "26156bcb-4775-5f66-a4aa-b5fd9ad3c34a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "624170b7-c364-5634-b8a5-b464680480ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "7e2af229-5140-5a43-b7fa-c4a5d33b65f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" :answer/id #uuid "4e1d4225-515a-5653-87ff-1da50cde4cf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "a40fc808-0e04-5dbc-af3a-696d4fcf0822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b43d465-a0b4-535f-8262-fab967b6ad8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170350/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-170350/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-170350/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-170350/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-170350/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" "q-170350/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170350" :question/id #uuid "8624b867-0c46-5fb9-bd50-4d5ac46cd2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-170350" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170350/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170350"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8f291ce2-f97d-52e7-a1bf-afea2d925af6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "3ba6da0c-ba6c-56e5-9f01-2110340f21d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "5cea4abd-4e1a-5956-bb45-ae956c7dd9a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "110c18c9-6b7c-568b-8bd1-a7b91291f6d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f8f0d09d-f72a-524d-9024-ec3605a9b2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "31d4cfb6-3e51-5560-b60c-60851c45a897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-7244/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-7244/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-7244/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-7244/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-7244/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-7244/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-7244" :question/id #uuid "ebf541e4-55e3-5021-83f0-ef5b1063441e" :question/math-academy-id "q-7244" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/8bde9a87a568654933ce9996f23605ea1219e2ff8416d9d5097971d9b6ef6087.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Hence, $c=-1.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-1 & =4 \\\\ k-1 & =4 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=5-(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=5{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-7244/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-7244"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
